WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.840
TRACE retrieves objects from clutter under arm occlusion.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.840
Withdrawing the arm restores visibility, but interrupts manipulation.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.840
The teacher plans once in simulation.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.840
The plan-conditioned student uses memory and partial feedback.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.840
Pushing continues without sensing retractions.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.840
TRACE achieves ninety percent success in hardware trials.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.840
Pushing needs no online search or simulation.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.840
The plan stays fixed; actions adapt as objects move.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:29.840
Demonstrations.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.076
Here is TRACE in real time, on a single
scene.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.068
The teacher has already planned once in
simulation.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.969
The student uses that plan, partial

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:44.616
observations, and memory to guide each
push.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.822
It keeps pushing even when the arm blocks
the camera.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.456
Only after pushing does the arm withdraw
for a final graspability check.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.871
The robot then grasps the exposed target
and retrieves it.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.957
Now, two more scenes at four times speed,
shown one at a time.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.144
Each scene starts with its own plan, which
stays fixed during execution.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.862
The actions adapt as contact moves

00:01:24.862 --> 00:01:27.224
objects away from their predicted
positions.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.750
Memory supports pushing under partial
visibility, without

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.400
online teacher queries, simulation, or
sensing retractions.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.398
For comparison, Teacher Replay executes a
fixed action sequence.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.308
Without object feedback, it cannot correct

00:01:45.308 --> 00:01:48.316
deviations. Here, the target remains
ungraspable.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.869
The online teacher repeatedly retracts the

00:01:51.869 --> 00:01:54.866
arm to recover a complete scene
observation.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.931
It reaches ninety five percent success in
the hardware benchmark.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.650
Those repeated sensing motions add time.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.990
P M B S combines tree search with batched
simulation to choose each push.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.472
Planning and repeated scene reacquisition
increase total time.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.422
In this trial, it exhausts its push budget
without retrieving the target.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.095
Spiral uses a target centered pushing
heuristic. It is fastest on average,

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.043
but less reliable in tight clutter, with
frequent workspace violations.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.344
Across forty hardware trials, TRACE
achieves ninety percent success.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.056
No workspace violations or sensing
retractions

00:02:45.056 --> 00:02:48.155
during pushing were observed in these
trials.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.961
TRACE is two point nine times faster end
to end than the online teacher.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.840
Plan conditioned retrieval.
